\id MAT Table alignment tests
\h Tables
\toc1 Table alignment tests
\toc2 Tables
\toc3 Tbl
\mt1 Table alignment
\c 1
\p
\v 1 Each table below exercises one kind of cell alignment. The cell text says where it should sit: “start” means flush left in LTR and flush right in RTL; “end” is the opposite edge; “centre” is centred in its column.
\s1 1. Start (th/tc), single-line cells
\tr \th1 Head1 \th2 Head2 \th3 Head3
\tr \tc1 start \tc2 start \tc3 start
\tr \tc1 start start start \tc2 st \tc3 start start
\tr \tc1 st \tc2 start start start \tc3 st
\s1 2. End (thr/tcr), single-line cells
\tr \thr1 Head1 \thr2 Head2 \thr3 Head3
\tr \tcr1 end \tcr2 end \tcr3 end
\tr \tcr1 end end end \tcr2 en \tcr3 end end
\tr \tcr1 en \tcr2 end end end \tcr3 en
\s1 3. Centre (thc/tcc), single-line cells
\tr \thc1 Head1 \thc2 Head2 \thc3 Head3
\tr \tcc1 centre \tcc2 centre \tcc3 centre
\tr \tcc1 centre centre centre \tcc2 ce \tcc3 centre centre
\tr \tcc1 ce \tcc2 centre centre centre \tcc3 ce
\s1 4. One alignment per column (start, centre, end)
\tr \th1 Book \thc2 Chapters \thr3 Verses
\tr \tc1 Genesis \tcc2 50 \tcr3 1533
\tr \tc1 Ruth \tcc2 4 \tcr3 85
\tr \tc1 Song of Songs \tcc2 8 \tcr3 117
\s1 5. Mixed alignments within each column
\tr \tc1 start start start start \tcc2 centre centre centre \tcr3 end end end end
\tr \tcc1 centre \tcr2 end \tc3 start
\tr \tcr1 end \tc2 start \tcc3 centre
\s1 6. Start, wrapping cells
\tr \th1 Key \th2 Description
\tr \tc1 start \tc2 start: a long cell whose text runs on for long enough that it has to wrap onto a second line and probably a third, ending here.
\tr \tc1 start start \tc2 start short
\s1 7. End, wrapping cells
\tr \thr1 Key \thr2 Description
\tr \tcr1 end \tcr2 end: a long cell whose text runs on for long enough that it has to wrap onto a second line and probably a third, ending here.
\tr \tcr1 end end \tcr2 end short
\s1 8. Centre, wrapping cells
\tr \thc1 Key \thc2 Description
\tr \tcc1 centre \tcc2 centre: a long cell whose text runs on for long enough that it has to wrap onto a second line and probably a third, ending here.
\tr \tcc1 centre centre \tcc2 centre short
\s1 9. Spanning cells
\tr \th1 Head1 \th2 Head2 \th3 Head3
\tr \tc1 start start \tc2 start start \tc3 start start
\tr \tc1-2 start (spans 1–2) \tc3 start
\tr \tcc1-3 centre (spans 1–3)
\tr \tc1 start \tcr2-3 end (spans 2–3)
\tr \tcr1-3 end (spans 1–3)
\tr \tc1-3 start (spans 1–3)
\p
\v 2 End of tests.
